Context from the neighbouring pages: Grothendieck works in an 'atelier', a combinatorial framework of 'figures' on a set M = M_0 ⊔ M_1. Here M_0 = L is an ordered set, thought of as points of a line. M_1 consists of bounded intervals I_{a,b}, thought of as edges. M carries an order ≤ and a face/incidence relation ⊴ (strict form ◁). Prefigures are Dreg*(M, ≤). Figures are finite subsets of M, well ordered by ≤ and closed under ⊴. In other words, finite 'polygons', meaning 1-dimensional subdivided paths on the line L.

The previous page explains the motive. For each prefigure Φ he wants a 'complementary' prefigure Φ'. With only bounded intervals, the boundaries satisfy only δΦ' ⊂ δΦ ∪ {ω_0, ω_1}, where ω_0, ω_1 are the extremal elements of L (if they exist). If L has no extremal elements, the complement leaves the class of prefigures altogether.

This page draws the conclusion. If one allows 'generalized' (half-infinite) polygons, the clean identity δΦ = δΦ' holds. This fails for the formalism of non-generalized polygons, where the common or non-common extremities spoil it.

He then enlarges M to M̂ = M̂_0 ⊔ M̂_1:
- M̂_0 = M_0 = L;
- M̂_1 = M_1 ⊔ M_1^- ⊔ M_1^+;
- M_1^- = {I_{<a} : a ∈ L not minimal};
- M_1^+ = {I_{>a} : a ∈ L not maximal}.

The new elements are formal 'graphic symbols'. Heuristically, I_{<a} stands for the ray of points x < a (or of all cells X < a), and I_{>a} for the ray beyond a. He defines:
- ∂I_{<a} = δI_{<a} = {a} (only one endpoint);
- support |I_{<a}| = L_{≤a};
- interior |I_{<a}|° = |I_{<a}| \ ∂I_{<a} = L_{<a};
- dually for I_{>a}.

Finally the face relations ⊴, ◁ (written ≤̊) are extended to M̂. The next page lists the new cases: I_{<a} ≤̊ I_{<b} ⟺ a ≤ b, and X ≤̊ I_{<a} ⟺ X ≤ a. It also notes that one never has I_{<a} ≤̊ X with X a bounded cell, even when ω_0 exists, so that I_{ω_0,a} ≠ I_{<a} even though their supports coincide.

In modern terms, this is passing from the cell structure of a bounded subdivided segment to the face poset of a point arrangement on a line. That face poset has vertices, bounded edges and unbounded rays. In it every 1-chain has a complement with the same boundary, a discrete one-dimensional Alexander-type complementarity. The purpose is to make the complementarity/duality formalism for 1-dimensional 'figures' symmetric and boundary-preserving.
